StyleSmuggler root cause: only sign directives that were explicitly deferred

Framework\Filter\Template signs "deferred" directives - those a child template hands up to
its parent - with a per-request value, and its second pass executes only signed directives.
A directive is judged deferred by testing whether processing left it unchanged:

    if ($result['directive'] === $result['output']) { ...sign it... }

That is also true for any directive the filter could not resolve. Framework\Filter\Template
has no blockDirective, so a {{block}} arriving through a customer field passes through
untouched, is mistaken for deferred, and is signed. Email\Model\Template\Filter does
implement blockDirective, so the parent then executes it.

This replaces the heuristic with an explicit declaration: Template::deferToParent() records
intent, and only recorded directives are signed. Email\Model\Template\Filter::inlinecssDirective()
declares itself in its isChildTemplate() branch - the only genuine deferral in the codebase.

The signature scheme itself is unchanged. The unchanged-output test is kept as a required
conjunct, so the set of signed directives is a strict subset of what was signed before;
nothing that was previously unsigned becomes signed.

Scope: this is the root cause only. The check-after-construct defects in BlockFactory and
UrlGeneratorFactory, and the report-file execution guards, are addressed by Adobe's
APSB26-146 patch; they are deliberately not duplicated here. The two patches touch disjoint
files and are intended to be applied together.

Origin: https://gist.github.com/pingiun/00cfbfdc3cf517807eb3b6bc24c7f295
Adapted for a Composer install: paths rewritten from the magento2 source layout
(app/code/Magento/Email, lib/internal/Magento/Framework) to vendor/magento/*, and the
upstream unit tests dropped - the vendor dist of TemplateTest.php differs from the source
repo copy, so those hunks do not apply here.

Applies -p1 from the project root. Verified against Magento 2.4.8-p5.

